  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published method paper model neural memory, hierarchical forecasting, and visual segmentation dynamics against experimental data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of explicit computational models to characterize how neural systems hold and predict temporal information. NeuroHorizon uses hierarchical prediction memory with scheduled sampling to forecast macaque and mouse neural population activity up to 1 s ahead. FDTest addresses memory directly, testing violations of a generalized fluctuation-dissipation theorem to quantify local memory effects in whole-brain models. Two further preprints address input and feedback structure. Sensory-realistic networks match human working-memory behavior better than abstract-input models, with the gain attributed mainly to input realism. A normative segmentation model couples feature and segment inference, and its recurrent feedback reproduces single-neuron gain and variability in visual cortex. The shared emphasis is on biologically grounded inputs, hierarchical memory, and quantitative tests of model dynamics against neural or behavioral data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-12"
}
